    %%% New outline, only review the lit on pronouns and factors that actually go into my final models

    Examinations of structural constraints on the subject pronoun paradigm have generally not been very revealing save for the effects of the verb type following the subject pronoun.
    Referent gender has been examined a number of times in light of 3sg and 3pl subject pronouns typically being marked for gender in French varieties outside of Louisiana.
    This has not held true within Louisiana for 3pl subject pronouns (\citeauthor{conwell_louisiana_1963}, \citeyear[p.~144]{conwell_louisiana_1963}; \citeauthor{papen_structural_1997}, \citeyear[p.~84]{papen_structural_1997}).
    Referent gender is variably relevant to 3sg in Louisiana in both French-oriented and Creole-oriented speech in that speakers of all varieties of F\&C may or may not mark gender in these subject pronouns (\citeauthor{conwell_louisiana_1963}, \citeyear[p.~144]{conwell_louisiana_1963}; \citeauthor{dajko_ethnic_2009}, \citeyear[p.~160]{dajko_ethnic_2009}; \citeauthor{klingler_if_2003}, \citeyear[pp.~170/209]{klingler_if_2003}; \citeauthor{mayeux_rethinking_2019}, \citeyear[p.~115]{mayeux_rethinking_2019}; \citeauthor{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985}, \citeyear[p.~170]{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985}; \citeauthor{phillips_etude_1936}, \citeyear[p.~43]{phillips_etude_1936}; \citeauthor{rottet_language_1995}, \citeyear[p.~128]{rottet_language_1995}; \citeauthor{smith_morphosyntactic_1994}, \citeyear[p.~47]{smith_morphosyntactic_1994}).
    Similarly different from French varieties outside of Louisiana is that referent animacy is more relevant in the latter.
    In Hexagonal French, for instance, \lexi{il} and \lexi{elle} regularly refer to both animate and inanimate referents, whereas this is not attested in Louisiana for French-oriented speakers who use \lexi{ça} for inanimate referents \textcite{brown_pronominal_1988},\footnote{
      \textcite[pp.~47-48]{smith_morphosyntactic_1994} and \textcite[p.~145]{conwell_louisiana_1963} both claimed to have documented the opposite behavior, more in line with other French varieties, but both also only provided examples that support the idea that only \lexi{ça} is used for 3sg.I referents.
      Alternatively, \textcite{brown_pronominal_1988} claimed that \lexi{ça} was used for animate referents, but the examples she provided had questionable interpretations.
      Her quantitative results were more mixed, but she collapsed 3pl and 3sg together for that analysis, making it a difficult result from which to draw conclusions.
    } although it is not unheard of for Creole-oriented speakers to use \lexi{li} for both animate and inanimate referents \parencite{klingler_if_2003}.
    A related exception was limited to some young speakers in Terrebonne-Lafourche who retained \lexi{eux}\footnote{
      This pronoun is often written in the literature as \orth{eusse} when pronounced with a final [s] as this final [s] is not generally attested in varieties of French outside of Louisiana where the only pronunciation of \lexi{eux} is [ø].
      This was not done here because because the process is regularized across derivative words (\foreign{e.g}, \lexi{ceux} as [søs]), and because there is historical evidence of the disjunctive pronoun \lexi{eux} being pronounced with a final [s] in Paris as recently as 1920 \parencite[p.~111]{bauche_langage_1928}.
    } in addition to \lexi{ça} for 3pl, using the former for animate referents and latter for inanimate \parencite[p.~219]{rottet_language_1995}.
    \lexi{Eux} was nearly absent in the present study, however.
    \textcite{brown_pronominal_1988} had also examined whether the syntactic position of co-referents predicted third person subject pronoun realizations but found this variable to not be significant (p.~168).

    % Following verb type has turned out to be meaningful
    The verb type for the verb following a subject pronoun has proven to be a more meaningful structural constraint, some findings have not been without their own methodological issues, though.
    Disjunctive pronouns were found to be present more often with verbs that express beliefs and opinions \parencite[p.~160]{dubois_attrition_2001}, but this is likely due to the general function of disjunctive pronouns of stressing contrasts, something that one would typically do when expressing beliefs and opinions.
    Null subject pronouns have also been found to be more common with reflexive verbs \parencite[Guilbeau, 1950, as cited in][p.~183]{rottet_language_1995}, a result that may perhaps be explained by the reflexive pronoun having co-reference with the subject pronoun, rendering the subject pronoun somewhat\footnote{
      This redundancy is more true for 1sg and 2sg than others person-number combinations as many of the latter call for the same reflexive pronoun.
      In Louisiana, even 2pl subject pronouns can take the same reflexive pronoun as all third person subject pronouns \parencite[p.~85]{papen_structural_1997}.
    } redundant.
    The phonetic form of first person subject pronouns has also been shown to be predictable based on the inflectional class of the following verb \parencite[pp.~14-15]{gudmestad_variationist_2022}, although this study is not concerned with phonetic variation, and no one has documented the same behavior for pronoun choice.

    % A more robust verb type finding focuses on the status of the verb as lexical, auxiliary, or modal, though
    A structural constraint on pronoun choice that is more directly relevant to the current study and that has been documented several times is the type of verb in terms of whether it is a lexical, a modal, or an auxiliary verb.
    \textcite{brown_pronominal_1988} looked at something like this in her analysis, finding that \lexi{ça} was preferred for third person before intransitive verbs, slightly dispreferred before transitive verbs, and dispreferred before modal verbs (pp.~173-174).
    This result is difficult to disentangle because of the conflation of \lexi{il} and \lexi{elle} with \lexi{ils} as the alternative pronoun realization but also because she sometimes interpreted the verb \lexi{être} \gloss{to be} as intransitive.
    The verb \lexi{être} was thus grouped with verbs such as \lexi{dormir} \gloss{to sleep} or perhaps \lexi{rester} \gloss{to stay}/\gloss{to live} without acknowledging that it may behave quite differently as a copula.
    On the other hand, \textcite{byers_defining_1988} compared the distribution of \lexi{ça} versus \lexi{ils} for 3pl relative to the following verb without abstracting to verb type.
    His results suggested that \lexi{ça} was preferred before \lexi{être}, \lexi{avoir} \gloss{to have}, and \lexi{aller} \gloss{to go}, whereas \lexi{ils} was preferred with all other verbs (p.~49).
    The common thread between these three verbs is that they are all auxiliary verbs that are, importantly, distinct from other auxiliary verbs in not always being modal as is the case with auxiliaries such as \lexi{pouvoir} \gloss{to be able to} and \lexi{devoir} \gloss{to have to}.

    % Social constraints that were modeled
    Sociolinguistic analyses of F\&C have been relatively rare compared to documentary work.
    For subject pronouns, \textcite{rottet_language_1995} and \textcite{dajko_ethnic_2009} provided the most robust sociolinguistic work in terms of subject pronoun choice, both examining Terrebonne-Lafourche, but \textcite{gudmestad_variationist_2022} also presented sociolinguistic analyses of phonetic variation in 1sg subject pronouns, again examining Terrebonne-Lafourche.
    All such sociolinguistic analyses focused entirely on speech that was said to be French rather than Creole, hence pronouns that are included in the present study that are traditionally described as Creole pronouns do not really factor into previous studies, but their results are enlightening for showing the sort of work subject pronouns can do in any case.

    No studies have considered exposure to institutional French as a factor, as was done in the present work, nor even factors that are likely to be associated with exposure to institutional French such as occupation or educational attainment.
    Gender and age, however, have been examined in the literature.
    Gender has mostly proved to be insignificant, although there have been some exceptions.
    \textcite{rottet_language_1995} found that men and women both primarily used \lexi{je} for 1sg and \lexi{vous-autres} for 2pl, but men were more inclined to use null pronouns for the former and either \lexi{vous} or \lexi{tu} for the latter (pp.~178/193).
    Likewise, gender was meaningful for phonetic variation in 1sg subject pronouns \parencite[pp.~14-15]{gudmestad_variationist_2022}.
    Some results for age have also proven to be significant.
    Those over 50 slightly dispreferred using \lexi{ça} compared to an aggregate of 3sg and 3pl pronouns (\foreign{i.e.}, \lexi{il} + \lexi{elle} + \lexi{ils}) in the parishes of Avoyelles and Assumption, whereas those under 50 slightly preferred \lexi{ça} \parencite[p.~178]{brown_pronominal_1988}.
    This shift over time in favor of \lexi{ça} for 3pl was also present in Terrebonne-Lafourche, but there, the much larger shift was in favor of \lexi{eux} for 3pl.
    Additionally in these latter parishes, younger speakers were more likely to use null pronouns for 1sg, although all speakers preferred \lexi{je}, and to use \lexi{tu} for 2sg.V.
    Another rather unexpected result there included the middle age group (\foreign{i.e.}, 30-54) using \lexi{tu} almost as much as \lexi{vous-autres} for 2pl, whereas all age groups preferred the latter otherwise \parencite[pp.~178/193/197/207]{rottet_language_1995}.
    It should be noted, however, that \textcite{rottet_language_1995} included speakers of different fluency levels, which appeared to be correlated with age (p.~163), making it difficult to disentangle how much of this variation was related specifically to age.
    
    Finally, and most importantly, ethnicity and race have been examined in the literature, although none of this work has been framed as examinations of racialization.
    Race, in terms of the broader US categories of White and Black, has often been implicated in studies of what has been conceptualized as Creole.
    These have not been variationist sociolinguistic studies, so the comments on race were generally more holistic and impressionistic.
    In Breaux Bridge, for example, \textcite{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985} described White speakers of Creole as using a more decreolized version of the language, which would include things like grammatical gender and a lack of postnominal definite articles (p.~41).
    One could likewise assume that this would mean that they were more likely to use French-oriented subject pronouns, but this was not something \textcite{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985} considered.
    \textcite{klingler_if_2003} did consider pronouns in the Creole of Pointe Coupee in suggesting that gender was more likely to be marked in any type of pronoun for White speakers, giving the example of \lexi{avec elle} \gloss{with her} rather than \lexi{avec li} \gloss{with him/her} (p.~117).
    \lexi{Elle}, in this case, would always have a feminine referent whereas \lexi{li} could have either a feminine or masculine referent.

    More robust analyses came once again from \textcite{rottet_language_1995} and \textcite{dajko_ethnic_2009}, although since these were dealing with ``French'' and Indians versus Cajuns, the factor was conceptualized as ethnicity rather than race.
    Both researchers certainly provided hints that these two groups are racialized, with Indians being given a lower status, but presumably, both categories and their related language varieties were White enough to not entail thinking about them in terms of race.
    Rottet and Dajko worked in the same region of Louisiana, but they did not examine exactly the same subject pronouns.
    Those that were shared included 2pl and 3pl, and with some caveats, 1sg.
    For 2pl, \textcite{rottet_language_1995} found an overall preference for \lexi{vous-autres} for both groups, but Cajuns were otherwise much more split between \lexi{vous-autres}, \lexi{vous}, and \lexi{tu} (p.~193).
    By \citeauthor{dajko_ethnic_2009}'s (\citeyear{dajko_ethnic_2009}) time, ethnicity was no longer significant for 2pl with both groups greatly preferring \lexi{vous-autres} (p.~154).
    For 3pl, both researchers found \lexi{eux} to be quite common across the board, but there were differences in how frequently other 3pl pronouns were used.
    In the earlier study, Cajuns actually made slightly more use of \lexi{eux-autres} but also made ample use of \lexi{ils} and \lexi{ça}, no pronoun form accounting for more than 37\% of occurrences \parencite[p.~207]{rottet_language_1995}.
    \textcite{dajko_ethnic_2009}, on the other hand, found \lexi{eux} to be preferred by both groups, but Cajuns still showed some non-negligible use of the other pronouns with the exception of those Cajuns who resided in the town of Golden Meadow who almost exclusively used \lexi{eux}.
    For Indians, by contrast, the only other 3pl pronoun that was used noticeably was \lexi{ça} (pp.~144-145).
    There is a caveat for both researchers examining 1sg because they used different levels for this variable.
    Rottet divided it into \lexi{je}, \lexi{moi je}, \lexi{moi}, and null, whereas Dajko divided it into \lexi{je}, \lexi{ej}, and null.
    If one excludes disjunctive pronouns and phonetic variation, however, both become a comparison between \lexi{je} and null, in which case both Indians and Cajuns greatly preferred \lexi{je} in both studies with Indians in the earlier study simply using a null pronoun more often than Cajuns (16\%) (\citeauthor{dajko_ethnic_2009}, \citeyear[pp.~156-158]{dajko_ethnic_2009}; \citeauthor{rottet_language_1995}, \citeyear[p.~178]{rottet_language_1995}).
    Their results for 1sg were in line with \citeauthor{gudmestad_variationist_2022}'s (\citeyear{gudmestad_variationist_2022}) much more recent study that likewise showed \lexi{je} to be heavily preferred to null for Indians alone.\footnote{
      \textcite{gudmestad_variationist_2022} did not examine any other group of speakers, and their analysis was of phonetic variation in \lexi{je}, so as with \citeauthor{dajko_ethnic_2009}'s (\citeyear{dajko_ethnic_2009}) use of the two phonetic variants of \lexi{je} and \lexi{ej} in her study, the current discussion is based on collapsing \citeauthor{gudmestad_variationist_2022}'s phonetic variants all into \lexi{je} save for the null variant.
    }

    The remaining subject pronouns that were examined were 2sg.V, 3sg.F, and 1pl.
    \textcite{rottet_language_1995} described the results for 2sg.V as significant for ethnicity.
    However, the rates of usage for the variants were almost identical between Indians and Cajuns (38\% vs 34\% for \lexi{vous}, 59\% vs 62\% for \lexi{tu}, and 3\% vs 4\% for \lexi{vous-autres}, respectively) (p.~197).
    If the statistical analysis was correctly carried out and did in fact lead to a significant result, the effect size is likely to have been inconsequential nonetheless.
    For 3sg.F, while \textcite{dajko_ethnic_2009} found both groups to greatly prefer \lexi{elle}\footnote{
      She in fact split \lexi{elle} into the phonetic variants [ɛl] and [al] while still including the lexical variants of \lexi{il} and null in the same analysis, but I am discussing \lexi{elle} as a lexical variant here (\foreign{i.e.}, I am combining its phonetic variants for the sake of this discussion).
    } but Indians also used a notable amount of \lexi{il} (15.2\%) (p.~162), which other French speakers in Louisiana and elsewhere would relegate to masculine referents only.
    She also considered 1pl but came up with a result that was not significant for ethnicity with both groups greatly preferring \lexi{on} to almost the complete exclusion of other variants \parencite[pp.~149-152]{dajko_ethnic_2009}.

    What may not be immediately apparent in these results is that Indians and Cajuns in Terrebonne-Lafourche almost entirely agreed on their preferred subject pronouns even if there was statistically significant variation in the frequency of the lesser used variants.
    Table \ref{tab:preferred_pronouns_tl} summarizes this pattern.
    As already mentioned, both \textcite{rottet_language_1995} and \textcite{dajko_ethnic_2009} discussed the social position of Indians in a way that is suggestive of them having a lower status than Cajuns, but if the situation reaches the level of these groups being racialized, Indians and Cajuns do not appear to be heavily marked by their subject pronoun usage in F\&C.
    One may still identify them through their subject pronouns, but the contrast, being only in the rates of usage of dispreferred pronouns, is not particularly stark.

    \begin{table}[tbhp]
      \caption{The most frequent subject pronouns in Terrebonne-Lafourche by ethnicity}
      \label{tab:preferred_pronouns_tl}
      \centering
      \begin{tabular}{l | l l | l l}
                        & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Indian}                                     & \multicolumn{2}{| c}{Cajun}                                    \\
        Subject Pronoun & \textcite{rottet_language_1995} & \textcite{dajko_ethnic_2009} & \textcite{rottet_language_1995} & \textcite{dajko_ethnic_2009} \\
        \hline
        1sg             & je                              & je                           & je                              & je                           \\
        2sg.V           & tu                              &                              & tu                              &                              \\
        3sg.F           &                                 & elle                         &                                 & elle                         \\
        1pl             &                                 & on                           &                                 & on                           \\
        2pl             & vous-autres                     & vous-autres                  & vous-autres                     & vous-autres                  \\
        3pl             & eux                             & eux                          & eux-autres                      & eux                          \\
      \end{tabular}
    \end{table}

    Two final notes can be made about how subject pronouns have been shown to be related to ethnicity or race in the literature.
    One is that 1sg subject pronouns have been given phonetic treatments several times, already discussed briefly above, or have otherwise been commented on in terms of phonetics in light of the considerable number of variants that are possible.
    These have once again all been focused on Terrebonne-Lafourche, where the [s] realization and especially the [z] realization of \lexi{je} have been particularly linked to Indians, both of which represent depalatalized versions of the typical [ʃ] and [ʒ] realizations, respectively (\citeauthor{dajko_ethnic_2009}, \citeyear[pp.~121-122]{dajko_ethnic_2009}; \citeauthor{papen_structural_1997}, \citeyear[p.~82]{papen_structural_1997}; \citeauthor{rottet_language_1995}, \citeyear[pp.~127/176]{rottet_language_1995}).
    While phonetic variation is not part of the present analysis, this does show that aspects of subject pronouns can reach a level of association with an ethnicity such that this association is apparent to anyone who spends considerable time with those subject to the association.

    The other final note is that examinations of the subject pronouns of Creoles have been entirely limited to Creole-oriented speech save for one exception.
    \citeauthor{wendte_tale_2020}'s (\citeyear{wendte_tale_2020}) work was a discourse analysis of Creoles in Louisiana and Texas rather than a variationist analysis, so neither subject pronouns or any other more precise structural linguistic features were part of his work, but he did not limit participation in the study to Creoles who spoke Creole as described by linguists structurally but rather who spoke whatever speech variety the participants referred to as Creole (pp.~113-119).
    The result is that much of the speech that he presented from participants showed that Creoles very much do use subject pronouns that are traditionally described in the literature as French rather than only Creole pronouns.
    The group of speakers who were only quoted using French-oriented pronouns consisted of 5 Texans and 14 Louisianians, the latter from the parishes of Acadia, Evangeline, Lafeyette, and St Landry, all of which are parishes where participants in the present study resided during the study except Evangeline which was only a parish where one current participant was raised. 
    Some other participants in \citeauthor{wendte_tale_2020}'s (\citeyear{wendte_tale_2020}) study even mixed French and Creole pronouns in their speech.
    This group consisted of 4 Texans and 4 Louisianians, the latter from the parishes of Lafayette, St Landry, and St Martin, which are not only parishes relevant to the current study's participants' life histories but are in fact the primary parishes targeted here.
    The data \textcite{wendte_tale_2020} presented therefore indicates that one should not expect all Creoles to use Creole subject pronouns, but it does not resolve whether the overall tendency is towards one set of pronouns or another.

    In sum, there exist a very wide range of possibilities for subject pronouns in F\&C.
    These have been described extensively in documentary studies of French and Creole as well as in sociolinguistic studies to an extent.
    The type of verb following the subject pronoun is a common structural constraint that has been found for 3pl pronouns, while gender and age have had some significant results.
    Exposure to institutional French has, on the other hand, not been examined at all, nor have the related social variables of occupation, education, and socioeconomic class, at least in part because they have not been seen as meaningful variables in rural Louisiana communities.
    Race has been commonly commented on in studies of what is conceptualized as Louisiana Creole but not French where ethnicity has instead been the framework of choice.
    Two of the most robust variationist studies, namely \citeauthor{rottet_language_1995}'s (\citeyear{rottet_language_1995}) and \citeauthor{dajko_ethnic_2009}'s (\citeyear{dajko_ethnic_2009}), have in fact focused on ethnicity.
    Their studies, as well as many others that have given treatments of subject pronouns, have been centered on Terrebonne-Lafourche, which is rather far to the southeast of the target area for the present study.
    Nonetheless, previous work has established that subject pronouns can do the work of delineating speakers at various social levels even if there are some minor indications in \citeauthor{wendte_tale_2020}'s (\citeyear{wendte_tale_2020}) study that at least Creoles make use of a variety of subject pronouns rather than consistent set of subject pronouns.
    Thus, the research question for this chapter is the following:

    \begin{itemize}
      \item[RQ1:] Do F\&C subject pronouns vary systematically between Creole and Cajun speakers?
    \end{itemize}

    % -----------------------------------------------------------
    % Everything lit review below here to be either moved to methods or removed completely

