    \end{table}

    \begin{figure}[tbhp]
      % \captionsetup{font={footnotesize}}
      \centering
      \caption{3pl pronouns by following verb type}
      \label{fig:3pl_predtype}
      <<fig.height = 0.3 * textheight>>=
      plotsPredicate$thirdPl
      @
    \end{figure}

    A somewhat unexpected structural result was that the odds of producing \lexi{je} for 1sg were higher before auxiliaries than \lexi{mo} (intercept $\approx$ \Sexpr{Round(logitModelSummaries$firstSg$coefficients["mo~(Intercept)", "Estimate"])}; coefficient $\approx$ \Sexpr{Round(logitModelSummaries$firstSg$coefficients["mo~PredTypeauxiliary", "Estimate"])}; $P <$ \Sexpr{Ceiling(logitModelSummaries$firstSg$coefficients["mo~PredTypeauxiliary", "Pr(>|z|)"])}) or a null pronoun (intercept $\approx$ \Sexpr{Round(logitModelSummaries$firstSg$coefficients["ø~(Intercept)", "Estimate"])}; coefficient $\approx$ \Sexpr{Round(logitModelSummaries$firstSg$coefficients["ø~PredTypeauxiliary", "Estimate"])}; $P <$ \Sexpr{Ceiling(logitModelSummaries$firstSg$coefficients["ø~PredTypeauxiliary", "Pr(>|z|)"])}) as this is not a constraint that had been previously found.
    The most thorough structural investigation of 1sg subject pronouns, \textcite{gudmestad_variationist_2022}, looked at phonetic variation rather than lexical variation as is done here.
    They examined verb inflection classes rather than the lexical categories of verbs and had mixed results.
    Null pronouns were more likely than the [ʃ] realization of \lexi{je} before \lexi{-er} verbs but less likely than the [ʒ] realization in the same context (pp.~14-15).\footnote{
      While they did also look at \lexi{mo}/\lexi{moi}, it was somewhat tangential as it is not a phonetic variant of \lexi{je}.
    }
    The present study thus adds to the overall picture for structural variation of 1sg subject pronouns in Louisiana and to null pronouns in general.
    Indeed, a pattern found in studies of null pronouns in other languages -- generally fully pro-drop languages -- is that null pronouns are more likely in linguistic contexts where the verbal morphology already disambiguates the subject on its own \parencite{hochberg_functional_1986}, but for the speakers in the present data, the verbal morphology is more fleshed out for auxiliary verbs than non-auxiliaries, meaning it was before verbs that do not at all disambiguate the subject that null pronouns were preferred here, contrary to what has been found in other languages.

    The remaining factors that were modeled were all social factors, two of which proved to be quite impactful on subject pronoun realization, institutional French exposure and ethnicity, and another that impacted 2sg.T and 3pl pronouns, that being age.
    A major gap exists in birth years between the two youngest participants, both born in \Sexpr{max(participants$`Birth Year`)}, and the others in that the youngest of the remaining participants was born in \Sexpr{sort(participants$`Birth Year`)[length(participants$`Birth Year`) - 2]}, a difference of \Sexpr{max(participants$`Birth Year`) - sort(participants$`Birth Year`)[length(participants$`Birth Year`) - 2]} years.
    In contrast, the other \Sexpr{nrow(participants) - 2} participants had birth years ranging from \Sexpr{max(sort(participants$`Birth Year`)[1:(length(participants$`Birth Year`) - 2)])} to \Sexpr{max(sort(participants$`Birth Year`)[1:(length(participants$`Birth Year`) - 2)])} with almost all gaps being one year and the maximum gap being \convertSmallNumber{\Sexpr{max(diff(sort(participants$`Birth Year`)[1:(length(participants$`Birth Year`) - 2)]))}} years.
    While this caveat should be kept in mind, these outliers are not likely to distort the statistical results to a huge degree considering that, with and without the two youngest participants, the means are nearly identical (\Sexpr{round(mean(participants$`Birth Year`))} and \Sexpr{round(mean(sort(participants$`Birth Year`)[1:(length(participants$`Birth Year`) - 2)]))}, respectively) the medians are identical (\Sexpr{median(participants$`Birth Year`)}) and the standard deviations are not exceptionally different (\Sexpr{round(sd(participants$`Birth Year`))} and \Sexpr{round(sd(sort(participants$`Birth Year`)[1:(length(participants$`Birth Year`) - 2)]))}, respectively).

    For age, then, as birth years were converted for modeling, there is some evidence of a shift from a predominance of \lexi{tu} for 2sg.T to \lexi{to} as well as from a predominance of \lexi{ils/ça} to \lexi{yé}, as can be seen in Figures \ref{fig:2sg_age} and \ref{fig:3pl_age}, respectively.
    What is also apparent from the figures is that there seems to be an interaction with ethnicity and age, as well.
    Of those who produced \lexi{to}, almost all were Creoles, and almost all of those tokens produced by Cajuns came from Errol of \Sexpr{participants[participants$Name == "Errol Stoufle", "Raised (town)"]} where Creole-oriented speech has long been documented.
    Likewise, only two Cajuns produced \lexi{yé} for 3pl: Errol once again and Elizabeth.
    The latter was born and still resided in the town of \Sexpr{participants[participants$Name == "Elizabeth Naquin", "Raised (town)"]}, a place where it has been noted that ``Blancs''\footnote{
      White people
    } are known to speak Louisiana Creole \parencite[p.~40]{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985}.
    Outside of these two exceptions, what the results for age more likely represent is evidence that Creoles are more likely to pass on F\&C than Cajuns.
    Among the participants in this study, Creoles had generally less education and tended towards blue collar occupations, suggesting a lower socioeconomic class, which is not very disimilar from the case of the Indians of Terrebonne-Lafourche, who were poorer and more isolated than the Cajuns there, and who were likewise documented as more readily passing on the language in the 1990s \parencite[pp.~64/310]{rottet_language_1995}.
    This pattern of isolated, low status groups passing being more likely to pass on minority languages is perhaps a generalizable phenomenon.

