        plotsModelAge$thirdPl
        @
      \end{minipage}

      {\footnotesize
      Predicted values all come from holding all other factors constant at their reference levels.
      }
    \end{figure}

    Exposure to institutional French, or in other words having been in a French classroom, was much more consistently a statistically significant factor in the models.
    The subject pronouns with higher odds of being produced if one had institutional French exposure included \lexi{je} for 1sg, \lexi{on} for 1pl, and \lexi{tu} for impersonal.
    For expletive pronouns, while there was not a signficant result when comparing \lexi{ça} to a null pronoun, \lexi{il} did have significantly higher odds than a null pronoun.
    For 3pl, the odds of producing \lexi{ils} over \lexi{ça} also increased with exposure to institutional French.
    All of these subject pronouns -- \lexi{je}, \lexi{on}, \lexi{tu}, and \lexi{ils} -- are the the norm for what is taught in French classrooms, whereas their alternatives -- \lexi{mo}, \lexi{no}, \lexi{to}, and \lexi{ça}, respectively -- are not.
    Even for expletive pronouns, \lexi{il} is what is taught in the classroom, but while null is found throughout the French-speaking world in expletive contexts, and hence possible to hear in a French classroom, \lexi{ça} appears to be unique to Louisiana.
    A rather straightforward explanation that can be drawn from this is that the formality of the setting has influenced speakers' F\&C.
    This would align anecdotally with the apparent linguistic insecurity that I found when talking to some people in the community both among Cajuns and Creoles.

