    While a classroom can be an influencing factor in one's speech, making it a good candidate for explaining the results for institutional French exposure here, there is again a socioeconomic component.
    In the case of 3pl pronouns, institutional French exposure not only increased the odds of producing \lexi{ils} relative to \lexi{ça} but also decreased the odds of producing \lexi{yé} relative to \lexi{ça}, neither \lexi{yé} nor \lexi{ça} being pronouns that would ever be heard in a French classroom to express 3pl, although the latter would still be heard as a demonstrative pronoun.
    This means that the language used in the classroom cannot be directly influencing a preference for \lexi{ça} over \lexi{yé} like it can for a preference for \lexi{ils} over \lexi{ça}.
    However, as Figure \ref{fig:education_by_institutionalFr} shows, there's a relatively clear relationship between having a college education and having been exposed to institutional French (Fisher's exact: $P <$ \Sexpr{Ceiling(indEducInstFr$p.value)}).
    This does not mean that exposure to institutional French has no impact on speakers outside of the effects of socioeconomic class but rather that \lexi{ça} is more prevalent to begin with among those who are either from a higher socioeconomic class or are more likely to head towards a higher socioeconomic class than those who use \lexi{yé}.
    As will be seen for the effects of ethnicity, this aligns well with the racialization of Cajuns, the primary users of \lexi{ça}, as a higher status group than Creoles, the primary users of \lexi{yé},

    \begin{figure}[tbhp]
      \caption{Participants' exposure to institutional French relative to their education levels}
      \label{fig:education_by_institutionalFr}
      \centering
      <<fig.height = 0.3 * textheight, fig.width = 0.75 * textwidth>>=
      plotEduInst
      @
    \end{figure}
    
    The primary factor of interest in this analysis is ethnicity, meaning being Creole or Cajun, which was an even more consistently significant social factor than exposure to institutional French.
    To reiterate, the term \lexi{ethnicity} is used here not as a conclusion about the racialization of these categories but rather as an agnostic stand-in term.
    The models, summarized for ethnicity in Table \ref{tab:ethnicity_results}, show statistical significance for almost all pronouns, thus being Cajun shifts the odds towards \lexi{je} over \lexi{mo} for 1sg, \lexi{tu} over \lexi{to} for 2sg.T, \lexi{elle} over \lexi{li} for 3sg.F, \lexi{il} over \lexi{li} for 3sg.M, \lexi{on} over \lexi{no} for 1pl, \lexi{ils/ça} over \lexi{yé} for 3pl, and \lexi{tu} over \lexi{to} for impersonal.
    In other words, Cajuns show a preference for subject pronouns that have traditionally been described as part of Louisiana French and Creoles for those that have traditionally been described as part of Louisiana Creole.
    While on the surface this might seem like a result too obvious to be noteworthy, Creoles have been documented using the former variants \parencite{klingler_language_2003, klingler_probleme_2005, wendte_tale_2020}, and Cajuns, often equated to White people, have been documented using the latter variants \parencite{klingler_if_2003, neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985}.
    This was also the case among the participants here.
    For instance, both Terry and James self-identified as Creoles and used exclusively the Cajun-marked variants.
    In fact, not only did James not use \lexi{yé} for 3pl, he did not even use \lexi{ça}, preferring at all times to use the very standard French \lexi{ils}.
    On the other hand, as already mentioned, Errol and Elizabeth, both self-identified Cajuns, used almost exclusively Creole-marked variants.
    There were additionally speakers who used a mix of both classes of variants, sometimes as a demonstration of an ability to switch styles or code switch -- Elizabeth began her interview with two tokens of \lexi{je} for 1sg before settling in to exclusively using \lexi{mo} after the two minute mark, and Oliver L. explicitly talked about his ability to switch -- and sometimes as their typical speech patterns.
    Gene fits the second description in that all of his subject pronouns matched those traditionally described as French except for 3pl where he consistently used \lexi{yé}.
    Both this inconsistency between Creole and Cajun identity and speech patterns as well as the consistency uncovered by the models here are apparent enough to the community that metalinguistic commentary identifies both truths, as the discussion between Cynthia and Lucille below makes clear.
    \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
      \item Joshua (Interviewer): Est-ce que les Créoles parlent différemment des Cadiens? ... (45:03)\label{sent:creole_meta}
      \item[] Cynthia (Cajun): I went to the store.
            Mo'tais couru au store.
      \item[] Lucille (Cajun): Et toi t'as été plus élevée autour du créole.
            À la Ville Platte, les Noirs à la Ville Platte parlaient français cadien d'après ce que moi je me rappelle.
      \item[] Cynthia: Oui, il y a des Noirs qui parlent français comme nous-autres, mais il y a des Blancs qui parlent créole comme les Noirs.
      \item[] \longtrans{Joshua (Interviewer): Do Creoles speak different from Cajuns? ...}
      \item[] \longtrans{Cynthia (Cajun): I went to the store.
            I went to the store.}
      \item[] \longtrans{Lucille (Cajun): And you were more raised around Creole.
            In Ville Platte, Blacks in Ville Platte spoke Cajun French from what I remember.}
      \item[] \longtrans{Cynthia: Yes, there are Black who speak French like us, but there are Whites who speak Creole like Blacks.}]
    \end{enumerate}

