    Subject pronouns in F\&C can therefore be said to mark ones identity as Creole or Cajun.
    Creole pronouns match what has been described in the Louisiana Creole literature -- \lexi{mo}, \lexi{to}, \lexi{li}, \lexi{li}, \lexi{no}, \lexi{yé}, and \lexi{to} -- while Cajun pronouns match what has been described in the Louisiana French literature -- \lexi{je}, \lexi{tu}, \lexi{elle}, \lexi{il}, \lexi{on}, \lexi{ils/ça}, and \lexi{tu}.
    As the above discussion has perhaps made clear, this also means that subject pronouns are distinguished by the racial categories used broadly in the US: Black and White.
    Participants did not always give exactly one of these two categories when asked to self-identify racially,\footnote{
      The range of racial self-identifications given was in fact the reason this factor was not modeled.
    } but what they did provide can be remapped to these two categories for exploration in a relatively uncontroversial manner.
    If all those who used the words White or Caucasian in their racial self-identification are recoded as White, all those who used the words Black or African-American are recoded as Black, and all those who chose Creole, Cajun, or no race are excluded, the distribution of American races by Louisiana ethnicities looks like Figure \ref{fig:ethnicity_by_race}.
    What this suggests is that there exists a statistical association between these two categories (Fisher's exact: $P <$ \Sexpr{Ceiling(indEthnRace$p.value)}).
    In effect, one cannot claim to be Cajun and Black at the same time, and although one can do the inverse, it is not typical.

    \begin{figure}[tbhp]
      \caption{Participants' race relative to their ethnicity}
      \label{fig:ethnicity_by_race}
      \centering
      <<fig.height = 0.3 * textheight, fig.width = 0.75 * textwidth>>=
      plotParticipantsEthnRace
      @
    \end{figure}

    For the two participants who did claim White Creole identities, Deborah and Jerry, their subject pronouns were not those traditionally described as belonging to Louisiana Creole but rather those typical of descriptions of Louisiana French.
    Indeed, there is perhaps an even stronger association between American racial categories and subject pronoun realizations than for the related local categories of Creole and Cajun.
    Figures \ref{fig:subset_race_1sg} through \ref{fig:subset_race_3pl} show each of the subject pronoun types again but in this case with the subset of those who racially self-identified with broad American categories.
    They represent a clear pattern of Black people using more Creole variants and White people using more French variants.
    For a direct comparison, Table \ref{tab:percentage_ethn_race} shows that the percentage of French pronouns produced by those who identified as Cajun was nearly identical to the subset of those who identified as White, whereas the percentage of Creole pronouns for those who identified as Black was noticeably higher than for those who identified as Creole for every pronoun type.
    In essence, Creole pronouns do not just mark someone as Creole but mark them as Black and Creole.

