  \section{Discussion}
    \label{sec:pronoun_discussion}
    Whether one works under the framework of traditional sociolinguistic indicators and markers \parencite[pp.~237-238]{labov_sociolinguistic_1972}, indexical orders as applied to sociolinguistics \parencite{johnstone_mobility_2006, johnstone_indexicality_2008, silverstein_indexical_2003}, or markers and signifiers of racialized groups as used in sociological work (\foreign{e.g.}, \citeauthor{fassin_racialization_2011}, \citeyear[p.~420]{fassin_racialization_2011}; \citeauthor{holt_marking_1995}, \citeyear[p.~3]{holt_marking_1995}), the results of the above analyses provide evidence that subject pronouns in F\&C mark speakers as being Creole or Cajun, or more narrowly, as being a Black Creole or a White Cajun.
    There is a consistent association between subject pronouns used and speakers' identities, which is the minimal requirement in all of these frameworks.
    
    Although perceptions of subject pronouns were not quantitatively examined here, it can furthermore be said that this association does not pass unnoticed by Louisianians as their metalinguistic discourse shows, something that will be addressed further in Chapter \ref{chap:discourse}.
    The implication is that subject pronouns are at least Labovian markers or second-order indexical.
    This noticing is what allows subject pronouns in F\&C to out speakers, so to speak, preventing them from avoiding the social characteristics that others may associate with the racialized group to which they belong.
    That which is noticed may be any subject pronoun, as well, as they vary as a system of interrelated linguistic variables rather than varying independently.
    A Creole is likely to use not just \lexi{mo} but also \lexi{to}, \lexi{li}, \lexi{no}, and \lexi{yé}, whereas a Cajun is unlikely to use these pronouns, although neither of these potentialities is categorical.
    
    Subject pronouns in F\&C map onto broad American racialized categories perhaps even more so than they do onto the local categories of Creole and Cajun.
    In this sense, Creole and Cajun identities are simply the local expressions -- with their own idiosyncracies -- of macro-social categories that have been superimposed on Louisiana at least since the Civil War when the end of slavery triggered a series of campaigns to redefine terms such as \lexi{Creole} and \lexi{Cajun}.
    At times, this mapping combined with the association of ways of speaking with Creoles and Cajuns creates incongruities that can leave speakers visibly uneasy.
    In one poignant exchange in English with a man who, by appearance, might have been considered White and therefore Cajun, he admitted to me that he does not use his F\&C with many people even when given opportunities due to his way of speaking.
    While he did not pronounce the term people sometimes used as a label for his language, he made it clear that it would be referred to using a crude, racist term for Black people.
    Such is the degree of association between speech patterns and racialized categories that speakers may deny themselves the right to use their own language if their way of using it does not align with their identity.
    % The principle of multiple causes is the idea that more than one factor influences language variation patterns (Bayley 2002, as cited in Hazen 2014:12)

    It is also notable that linguistic marks of racialized identities can be observed even in a language that is in heavy decline and that finds very little public use.
    It has been suggested that such languages exhibit a lack of norms as there is no longer anyone to take up the function of setting and enforcing norms \parencite[p.~154]{dorian_language_1981}.
    In essence, where the dominant language speech community has many norm enforcement mechanisms, there are few if any for a declining language.
    Even the norm enforcement that occurs through regular interaction in the language with a variety of peers \parencite{milroy_language_1987} is no longer a given.
    This is true in Louisiana where speakers regularly report not having anyone with whom to speak F\&C, often as a result of the elders in their family passing away, suggesting that the home was the principal domain of use, although as will be seen in the next chapter, there is evidence that this is less likely to be true for Cajuns.
    % The implication is that these distinctions in language use existed
    
    What also makes the primacy of the home as the domain of use for F\&C less true for Cajuns is that they are more likely to be college educated as far as the data here is concerned.
    A difference in educational attainment itself is another indication that these groups are racialized, but in terms of language, it also means that Cajuns are more likely to have exposure to institutionalized varieties of French, meaning language varieties that are closely related to F\&C but which are, in a sense, non-indigenous to Louisiana.
    This is a situation that is not possible for many declining languages that have no closely related language varieties that exist outside of the community.
    Indeed, for F\&C, one might sooner call this a dialect in decline than a language in decline.
    
    The data here suggests that this exposure to institutionalized French is broadly predictive of subject pronoun realization, providing a potential norm enforcement mechanism for Cajuns but not for Creoles.
    While this exposure cannot be the cause for some pronoun choices among Cajuns due to not being part of the non-indigenous variety, such as \lexi{ça} for 3pl, the subject pronouns in the French used in classrooms are by and large the same as those used by Cajuns.
    At the very least, there is likely a reifying effect for the correctness and value of those subject pronouns with which Cajuns enter the French class that do align with what they are taught in that classroom.
    This is an experience and mechanism that is effectively absent for Creoles as they are less likely to go to college but also less likely to enter a French classroom with subject pronouns that align with those used in classroom speech.

    These two factors, the greater connectedness of Cajuns to other F\&C speakers and the greater likelihood that Cajuns will be exposed to institutionalized French, may be the mechanisms that have helped to maintain differences in the speech of Creoles and Cajuns.
    Both of these are indicative of racialization, especially the latter factor, but there is one additional factor to consider, that being a sort of fossilization of past distinctions based on these groups being racialized in the past when F\&C was more widely spoken in public.
    For those speakers who have truly had little to no use for F\&C outside of the home, their only linguistic influence is that of the elders in their homes who themselves may have had significant opportunities to use F\&C outside of the home.
    As will be discussed in Chapter \ref{chap:discourse}, a number of participants in this study themselves had experience with earlier times in Louisiana when segregation was more explicitly institutionalized.
    Some were able to recount what the area was like during the Jim Crow era.
    It goes without saying that those who raised them, those from whom they acquired F\&C, lived in a world where Creoles and Cajuns were heavily segregated in a number of ways.
    That segregation would have naturally led to differences in speech patterns between the relevant groups, differences that would get passed on to later generations.
    In the absence of a change towards greater integration that could facilitate a sharing a linguistic features -- in this case integration involving the continued use of F\&C -- differences would persist.
    With this in mind, the next chapter will examine personal social networks and the ethnic homophily present within them (\foreign{i.e.}, segregation) in the community of F\&C speakers today.